# UI Rules (Common File)

- For every new UI screen or component, prioritize a highly user-friendly, simple, and easy-to-understand experience.
- Treat this app as production-grade, not a college project; UI/UX quality must feel polished and modern by default.
- Follow one consistent brand design system across screens and components (color, spacing, typography, surfaces, and interaction patterns).
- Prefer a modern "AI-era" visual style: clean surfaces, strong contrast, calm backgrounds, and focused accent colors.
- As a default UI/UX decision rule, introduce icons, micro-animations, and color emphasis wherever they improve clarity, feedback, and usability.
- Use motion with purpose (state changes, success/error feedback, loading progress), not as decoration.
- Use icons to reduce reading burden and improve quick recognition, especially for low-literacy flows.
- Keep animation subtle and performant; avoid heavy or distracting effects in critical flows.
- Assume many users may have low digital literacy; keep flows short, obvious, and forgiving.
- Prefer clear labels, large tap targets, and minimal on-screen choices.
- Use plain language; avoid technical terms and complex wording.
- Keep important actions visible and consistent in placement.
- Reduce clutter: show only what users need for the current step.
- For role-based views (customer, agent, admin), display only role-relevant information.
- Recommend the most practical design pattern when multiple UI options exist, favoring clarity over visual complexity.
- For English/Hindi screens, keep wording short and equivalent across both languages.

- All UI components should come from a shared component layer (default name: `commonUI`).
- If a needed UI component does not exist, create it in `commonUI` first, then use it in screens/features.
- Avoid one-off inline UI components in feature screens when a reusable common component is possible.
- Keep component APIs simple and consistent so reuse stays easy.
- For screens with text inputs, use a shared keyboard-safe layout component (for example `KeyboardAwareScreen`) so keyboard behavior is consistent and UI is not hidden.

- UI must be theme-based:
  - Use shared theme tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radius, elevation).
  - Do not hardcode design values repeatedly in feature screens.
  - Prefer centralized theme definitions and consume them everywhere.
  - Do not hardcode `fontSize`, `fontFamily`, or `fontWeight` in screens/components; keep typography in theme tokens and reference from there.
  - Do not introduce ad-hoc color shades per screen; use only approved brand tokens from theme.

- UI text must be i18next-based:
  - All user-facing strings should come from i18next translation keys.
  - Avoid hardcoded visible text in components/screens except temporary placeholders during development.
  - Keep translation keys clear and reusable.

- When building or updating UI, prioritize reusable common components, theme consistency, and translation readiness by default.
